-- #1094, the SAME-TAIL half — caught by adversarial review of the first cut of the fix,
-- which closed only the closed~closed case and then CLAIMED to have closed the issue.
--
-- Both index rows end in the SAME physical effvar `e` but carry different prefixes.
-- `unifyRowN`'s same-tail arm DISCARDS the symmetric difference — its own comment says
-- so ("the atom evaporates … ACCIDENTAL leniency, NOT load-bearing"), which is fine for
-- an ARROW row and is exactly the widening invariance forbids for an INDEX. There is no
-- metavariable left to solve here (the tails are already the same cell), so
-- `<Stdout | e>` and `<e>` are two genuinely different rows.
--
-- Before the same-tail arm this file checked clean, and the shipped-`Async` version of
-- it (`launderAsync : Async <Stdout | e> a -> Async e a`) laundered <Stdout> all the way
-- into a `pureVal : Int` that printed, on both engines — i.e. #1094's flagship symptom
-- survived a fix that had already gone green on every gate.
--
-- The discriminating control is in effect_index_unrelated_ok.mdk: `relay`,
-- `pureBox` and especially `widen : Box <Stdout | e> Unit -> Box <Stdout | e> Unit` are
-- the same-tail case with EQUAL prefixes, whose symmetric difference is empty, and they
-- must stay accepted. Rejecting those instead would be the obvious over-correction.
data Box (e : Effect) a = MkBox (Unit -> <e> a)

launderBox : Box <Stdout | e> a -> Box e a
launderBox x = x
